### What is fail-fast vs fail-safe iteration?

Fail-fast and fail-safe describe how iterators respond when the underlying collection is modified during iteration.

| Feature | Fail-fast | Fail-safe (weakly consistent) |
|---|---|---|
| Collections | ArrayList, HashMap, HashSet, LinkedList | ConcurrentHashMap, CopyOnWriteArrayList |
| Throws CME | Yes (on structural modification) | Never |
| Mechanism | modCount check on every next() | Snapshot or tolerant traversal |
| Reflects changes | N/A (throws before seeing them) | May or may not see concurrent changes |
| Memory overhead | None | COWAL: full array copy; CHM: none |
| Thread safety | Not thread-safe | Thread-safe |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FailFastVsFailSafeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // FAIL-FAST: ArrayList
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001"));
        try {
            for (String acc : accounts) {
                if (acc.startsWith("CLOSED")) {
                    accounts.remove(acc); // CME!
                }
            }
        } catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
            System.out.println("ArrayList: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // FAIL-SAFE: ConcurrentHashMap
        ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        map.put("SAV2001", "Active");
        map.put("CLOSED-001", "Closed");
        map.put("CUR3001", "Active");

        for (var entry : map.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue().equals("Closed")) {
                map.remove(entry.getKey()); // no CME!
            }
        }
        System.out.println("ConcurrentHashMap after: " + map);

        // FAIL-SAFE: CopyOnWriteArrayList
        List<String> cowl = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001"));
        for (String acc : cowl) {
            if (acc.startsWith("CLOSED")) {
                cowl.remove(acc); // no CME! Iterator uses snapshot.
            }
        }
        System.out.println("COWAL after: " + cowl);
    }
}
```

```text
ArrayList: ConcurrentModificationException
ConcurrentHashMap after: {SAV2001=Active, CUR3001=Active}
COWAL after: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
```

### How does the modCount mechanism work?

Every `ArrayList`, `HashMap`, and `HashSet` maintains an internal `modCount` field. Every structural modification (add, remove, clear) increments this counter. When an iterator is created, it stores the current `modCount` as `expectedModCount`. On every call to `next()`, the iterator checks:

```java
// Simplified from OpenJDK ArrayList.Itr
public E next() {
    if (modCount != expectedModCount)
        throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
    // ... return next element
}

public void remove() {
    // ... remove the element
    expectedModCount = ++modCount; // keep in sync!
}
```

`iterator.remove()` is safe because it increments BOTH `modCount` and `expectedModCount`, keeping them synchronized.

### What are the safe ways to remove during iteration?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class SafeRemovalDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001", "CLOSED-002"));

        // Method 1: Iterator.remove()
        Iterator<String> it = accounts.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            if (it.next().startsWith("CLOSED")) {
                it.remove();
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Iterator.remove: " + accounts);

        // Reset for next demo
        accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001", "CLOSED-002"));

        // Method 2: removeIf (Java 8) – cleanest
        accounts.removeIf(acc -> acc.startsWith("CLOSED"));
        System.out.println("removeIf: " + accounts);

        // Method 3: Collect and remove after
        accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001", "CLOSED-002"));
        List<String> toRemove = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String acc : accounts) {
            if (acc.startsWith("CLOSED")) toRemove.add(acc);
        }
        accounts.removeAll(toRemove);
        System.out.println("removeAll: " + accounts);

        // Method 4: Stream filter (create new list)
        accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CLOSED-001", "CUR3001", "CLOSED-002"));
        List<String> active = accounts.stream()
            .filter(acc -> !acc.startsWith("CLOSED"))
            .toList();
        System.out.println("Stream filter: " + active);
    }
}
```

```text
Iterator.remove: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
removeIf: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
removeAll: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
Stream filter: [SAV2001, CUR3001]
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Assuming CME is only thrown in multi-threaded code

```java
// WRONG assumption: "I have one thread, so no CME"
for (String s : list) {
    list.remove(s); // CME! Even in single-threaded code!
}
// CME is about structural modification during iteration,
// not about multiple threads.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **CME is best-effort.** The fail-fast behavior is not guaranteed under all circumstances. It should not be relied upon for correctness – it is a debugging aid, not a contract.

- **Maps have the same behavior.** `HashMap.entrySet().iterator()` is fail-fast. `ConcurrentHashMap.entrySet().iterator()` is weakly consistent.

- **removeIf on Maps.** You can use `map.entrySet().removeIf(e -> e.getValue() < threshold)` to safely remove map entries during iteration.

- **for-each is syntactic sugar for Iterator.** `for (T t : collection)` compiles to `Iterator<T> it = collection.iterator(); while (it.hasNext()) { T t = it.next(); }`. That is why `collection.remove()` during for-each causes CME.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What causes ConcurrentModificationException?" – Structural modification (add/remove) during iteration, detected via modCount.
- "Is CME only in multi-threaded code?" – No, it happens in single-threaded code when modifying during for-each.
- "How do you safely remove during iteration?" – iterator.remove() or removeIf().
- "What is weakly consistent?" – Iterator may or may not reflect concurrent modifications.
